{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The CLI is defined and parsed with `clap`. No test is tagged to this requirement, and I think that is acceptable here.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml` depends on `clap = { version = \"4.6.7\", features = [\"derive\"] }`.\n- `src/main.rs` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`.\n- `struct Cli` derives `Parser` with `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]`. It is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.clap` at lines 10–11.\n- `enum Command` derives `Subcommand`. It declares these subcommands:\n  - `mdbook`, with a nested `supports` subcommand\n  - `setup`\n  - `status`\n  - `info`\n  - `author`\n  - a hidden `hook`\n  - `add-input`\n- The options on those subcommands use `#[arg(...)]`. `ArgGroup` expresses mutual exclusion and required groups: `author_target` for `author`, and `target` and `mode` for `add-input`. Other attributes cover the `author-evidence` alias, `conflicts_with`, `num_args`, and `value_names`.\n- `main` calls `Cli::parse().command` and dispatches on the result. This is also marked `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.clap`, at line 87.\n- There is no hand-rolled argument parsing.\n\n**Tests**\n- No test cites `requirements.cli.clap` directly.\n- `tests/cli.rs` runs the real binary on unknown commands and invalid options. It asserts failure and a `Usage: habeas` message, which is clap's error output. These tests cite `requirements.cli.invocation`.\n- `tests/commands.rs`, `tests/authoring_cli.rs` and `tests/live_bedrock.rs` exercise the parsed subcommands and flags end to end.\n- The requirement is structural: it says which library defines and parses the CLI. It is satisfied by the declarative derive code, and a dedicated test would add little. The existing tests exercise clap-generated behavior, such as usage errors and argument groups.\n\n**Gaps:** none acknowledged and none found. There are no FIXME markers.",
